{comment: Thomas Oliphant's words of 1862 ("Deck the hall"), not the later American rewrite.}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
[G]Deck the hall with boughs of [D]holly,
[G]Fa la la la la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
'[G]Tis the season to be [D]jolly,
[G]Fa la la la la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
[D]Fill the meadcup, [G]drain the [D]barrel,
[G]Fa la la, [Em]la la la, [D]la la la.
[G]Troll the ancient [C]Christmas [G]carol,
[C]Fa la la la [G]la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
[G]See the flowing bowl be[D]fore us,
[G]Fa la la la la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
[G]Strike the harp and join the [D]chorus,
[G]Fa la la la la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
[D]Follow me in [G]merry [D]measure,
[G]Fa la la, [Em]la la la, [D]la la la.
[G]While I sing of [C]beauty's [G]treasure,
[C]Fa la la la [G]la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
[G]Fast away the old year [D]passes,
[G]Fa la la la la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
[G]Hail the new, ye lads and [D]lasses!
[G]Fa la la la la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
[D]Laughing, quaffing [G]all to[D]gether,
[G]Fa la la, [Em]la la la, [D]la la la.
[G]Heedless of the [C]wind and [G]weather,
[C]Fa la la la [G]la, la la [D7]la [G]la.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: G}
